					<div class="summary" style="margin-bottom: 594.667px; opacity: 0;"><h1>Palazzo Reale hosts the first major retrospective dedicated to Chaïm Soutine (1893–1943) in an Italian museum.</h1></div>
			</div>
		</div>
</header>

<div class="contenuto-principale">
	<div class="testo-formattato">
		<div class="container">





				<div><p>Palazzo Reale hosts the first major retrospective dedicated to Chaïm Soutine (1893–1943) in an Italian museum.</p>

<p>The exhibition offers the public an important opportunity to explore the work of one of the most original figures in 20th-century painting, whose expressive power exerted a profound influence on subsequent generations, from Willem de Kooning and Jackson Pollock to Francis Bacon.</p>

<p>Chaïm Soutine is one of the most intense and restless voices in 20th-century art. Born into a poor Jewish family in the Russian Empire (in present-day Belarus), he grew up in a context marked by hardship and discrimination. In 1913, he moved to Paris, where he came into contact with the artistic scene of Montparnasse and the most advanced movements of his time, while remaining independent of avant-garde groups and manifestos. His engagement with the great European painting tradition, studied above all in the galleries of the Louvre, intersected with the progressive defining of a deeply personal language, founded on the force of color, the tension of the brushstroke, and the ability to transfigure observed reality.</p>

<p>Through landscapes, still lifes, and human figures, Soutine conveys a vision of reality permeated by a powerful and unstable energy. Forms seem to bend and come alive, while the painted surface becomes the space in which visual experience acquires a new intensity. In his portraits – just as in his famous depictions of animals and carcasses or his landscapes of Céret and southern France – the subject is transformed by painting, which amplifies its presence and expressive power.</p>

<p>The exhibition brings together around fifty major paintings from prestigious public and private collections, tracing the artist’s entire career and documenting the fundamental key moments of his art.</p>

<p><strong>An exhibition promoted by</strong> the Municipality of Milan – Culture and <strong>produced by</strong> Arthemisia Arte and Palazzo Reale.</p>

<p><strong>Curated by</strong>&nbsp;<span>Simonetta Fraquelli e Francesca Villanti</span></p></div>

